1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,573
Which parts of a chip belong together?

2
00:00:02,573 --> 00:00:05,146
That is the question this lab answers.

3
00:00:05,146 --> 00:00:08,803
A chip is made of millions of tiny parts called cells.

4
00:00:08,803 --> 00:00:14,491
Before a computer can arrange them, it needs to know which cells like to stay close.

5
00:00:14,491 --> 00:00:17,199
That liking is a number called affinity.

6
00:00:17,199 --> 00:00:22,752
In this lab you'll learn three ways to compute it, and see why the choice matters.

7
00:00:22,752 --> 00:00:27,256
A chip contains cells, the small building blocks of the circuit.

8
00:00:27,256 --> 00:00:30,001
Cells talk to each other through wires.

9
00:00:30,001 --> 00:00:32,957
We draw each cell as a dot, called a node.

10
00:00:32,957 --> 00:00:38,446
A line between two dots is an edge: it means the two cells are wired together.

11
00:00:38,446 --> 00:00:43,091
The number on an edge is its weight: how many wires join the pair.

12
00:00:43,091 --> 00:00:46,751
So A, B has weight five: five wires connect A and B.

13
00:00:46,751 --> 00:00:52,311
This drawing is called a graph, and it's the map every algorithm in this course

14
00:00:52,311 --> 00:00:52,944
works on.

15
00:00:52,944 --> 00:00:57,076
Cells that exchange many signals should sit close together.

16
00:00:57,076 --> 00:01:00,508
Short wires make the chip faster and save energy.

17
00:01:00,508 --> 00:01:05,480
Grouping close cells is called clustering, and each group is a cluster.

18
00:01:05,480 --> 00:01:10,383
Think of a seating plan: friends who chat a lot sit at the same table.

19
00:01:10,383 --> 00:01:14,445
Here, A, B and C form one group, and D and E form another.

20
00:01:14,445 --> 00:01:19,698
Only the red edges cross between the groups, and we want as few of those as

21
00:01:19,698 --> 00:01:20,328
possible.

22
00:01:20,328 --> 00:01:23,305
Affinity is a score for each pair of cells.

23
00:01:23,305 --> 00:01:27,250
A high score means these two should be in the same group.

24
00:01:27,250 --> 00:01:29,743
A low score means they can be apart.

25
00:01:29,743 --> 00:01:34,311
Clustering algorithms merge the pair with the highest score first.

26
00:01:34,311 --> 00:01:38,465
So the recipe for the score decides everything that follows.

27
00:01:38,465 --> 00:01:42,480
This lab compares three recipes on the same small example.

28
00:01:42,480 --> 00:01:46,634
Here is our example for the whole lab: five cells, A to E.

29
00:01:46,634 --> 00:01:50,717
The heavy edges are A, B and D, E, with weight five each.

30
00:01:50,717 --> 00:01:54,656
C sits in the middle, with thinner links to the others.

31
00:01:54,656 --> 00:02:00,672
Keep this picture in mind: we're going to give a score to every pair of cells in it.

32
00:02:00,672 --> 00:02:02,638
Recipe one is the simplest.

33
00:02:02,638 --> 00:02:07,297
The score of a pair is just the weight of the edge between them.

34
00:02:07,297 --> 00:02:12,101
Then we sort the pairs from highest to lowest, like a leaderboard.

35
00:02:12,101 --> 00:02:14,358
A, B and D, E are tied at five.

36
00:02:14,358 --> 00:02:20,182
When two scores are equal, we break the tie in alphabetical order, so A, B comes

37
00:02:20,182 --> 00:02:20,619
first.

38
00:02:20,619 --> 00:02:26,588
A fixed tie-break matters: it makes the computer give the same answer every single

39
00:02:26,588 --> 00:02:26,952
time.

40
00:02:26,952 --> 00:02:31,492
Recipe two adds an idea from everyday life: friends of friends.

41
00:02:31,492 --> 00:02:36,032
If two people share a close friend, they're probably close too.

42
00:02:36,032 --> 00:02:39,276
A and B are directly linked with weight five.

43
00:02:39,276 --> 00:02:41,438
They also share a neighbor: C.

44
00:02:41,438 --> 00:02:47,275
We add the weaker of the two links through C, which is one, so A, B rises to six.

45
00:02:47,275 --> 00:02:48,716
Why the weaker link?

46
00:02:48,716 --> 00:02:52,608
Because a chain is only as strong as its weakest link.

47
00:02:52,608 --> 00:02:56,893
With recipe two, A, B and D, E still lead, now at six.

48
00:02:56,893 --> 00:03:03,320
But look at A, C: on its own it was a weak edge of one, and now it jumps to five,

49
00:03:03,320 --> 00:03:04,272
thanks to B.

50
00:03:04,272 --> 00:03:10,302
Even pairs with no edge at all, like B, D, get a score, because they share a

51
00:03:10,302 --> 00:03:11,016
neighbor.

52
00:03:11,016 --> 00:03:16,443
EDA stands for electronic design automation: the software that designs chips.

53
00:03:16,443 --> 00:03:22,151
In EDA, affinity is the local preference: which cells belong together, before any

54
00:03:22,151 --> 00:03:23,984
rule about the whole chip.

55
00:03:23,984 --> 00:03:28,776
Bad affinity means bad merges, and fixing them later is much harder.

56
00:03:28,776 --> 00:03:32,335
Let's compute one score together, step by step.

57
00:03:32,335 --> 00:03:35,817
Step one: the direct edge A, C has weight one.

58
00:03:35,817 --> 00:03:39,527
Step two: A and C have one neighbor in common, B.

59
00:03:39,527 --> 00:03:45,130
The links through B weigh five and four, so we keep the smaller one: four.

60
00:03:45,130 --> 00:03:48,916
Step three: add them up, one plus four makes five.

61
00:03:48,916 --> 00:03:54,216
A, C was weak alone, but becomes strong once its shared friend counts.

62
00:03:54,216 --> 00:03:59,009
With recipe two, our small graph gives ten pairs instead of six.

63
00:03:59,009 --> 00:04:02,453
Four of them have no wire at all between them.

64
00:04:02,453 --> 00:04:07,621
For example, B and D are not wired together, but both are wired to C.

65
00:04:07,621 --> 00:04:11,215
So B, D gets the weaker of those two links: two.

66
00:04:11,215 --> 00:04:16,008
It's like two people who have never met but share a best friend.

67
00:04:16,008 --> 00:04:19,752
Only pairs whose score would be zero are left out.

68
00:04:19,752 --> 00:04:23,719
Here is recipe two written as steps a computer can follow.

69
00:04:23,719 --> 00:04:27,139
First, every edge adds its weight to its own pair.

70
00:04:27,139 --> 00:04:32,132
Next, for every pair of nodes, look at each neighbor they have in common.

71
00:04:32,132 --> 00:04:36,988
For each shared neighbor, add the smaller of its two links to the pair.

72
00:04:36,988 --> 00:04:40,203
Keep every pair whose score ends up above zero.

73
00:04:40,203 --> 00:04:45,264
Finally, sort from highest to lowest score, and break ties alphabetically.

74
00:04:45,264 --> 00:04:50,092
Here's a second example that shows why the recipe matters.

75
00:04:50,092 --> 00:04:55,253
Take a tight triangle P, Q, R, with every edge at weight four.

76
00:04:55,253 --> 00:04:59,000
Next to it, a lone pair X, Y has weight five.

77
00:04:59,000 --> 00:05:03,412
Recipe one picks X, Y first, because five beats four.

78
00:05:03,412 --> 00:05:09,655
Recipe two gives P, Q four, plus four through R: eight, so P, Q goes first.

79
00:05:09,655 --> 00:05:14,400
Same graph, same merge rule, but a different first merge.

80
00:05:14,400 --> 00:05:18,246
On a real chip, one wire can connect many cells at once.

81
00:05:18,246 --> 00:05:23,053
Such a shared wire is called a net, and each cell it touches is a pin.

82
00:05:23,053 --> 00:05:28,340
Think of a group chat with fifty people: it says little about who is friends.

83
00:05:28,340 --> 00:05:31,911
A private message between two people says much more.

84
00:05:31,911 --> 00:05:37,062
Recipe three works the same way: each net shares its weight among its pins.

85
00:05:37,062 --> 00:05:41,457
The rule is the weight divided by the number of pins, minus one.

86
00:05:41,457 --> 00:05:47,088
Here, the enable and reset nets touch five cells each, so they give only a quarter

87
00:05:47,088 --> 00:05:47,980
to each pair.

88
00:05:47,980 --> 00:05:52,032
The private net between E and F gives its full weight, one.

89
00:05:52,032 --> 00:05:55,714
Now it's your turn to compute scores, in the browser lab.

90
00:05:55,714 --> 00:06:00,818
Click two nodes to pick a pair, even a pair with no edge, work out its score by

91
00:06:00,818 --> 00:06:02,174
hand, and type it in.

92
00:06:02,174 --> 00:06:05,210
The lab tells you right away if you're correct.

93
00:06:05,210 --> 00:06:10,572
You can also build your own ranking, one pair at a time, and change edge weights to

94
00:06:10,572 --> 00:06:12,381
see which pair merges first.

95
00:06:12,381 --> 00:06:14,965
The ten challenges go from easy to hard.

96
00:06:14,965 --> 00:06:20,133
If you get stuck, the breakdown button shows the calculation, but that answer no

97
00:06:20,133 --> 00:06:22,200
longer counts for the challenge.

98
00:06:22,200 --> 00:06:25,767
If you like programming, the implement track is for you.

99
00:06:25,767 --> 00:06:30,862
First, print both rankings with the reference program, for the starter graph and

100
00:06:30,862 --> 00:06:32,646
then for the priority graph.

101
00:06:32,646 --> 00:06:36,085
Then write your own three scoring functions in Python.

102
00:06:36,085 --> 00:06:38,506
Finally, run the checker on your file.

103
00:06:38,506 --> 00:06:43,283
It compares your answers with the correct ones, and it feeds the edges in a

104
00:06:43,283 --> 00:06:47,232
scrambled order, so a missing tie-break shows up as a failure.

105
00:06:47,232 --> 00:06:49,724
Watch out for four common mistakes.

106
00:06:49,724 --> 00:06:52,785
Counting an edge twice, once from each end.

107
00:06:52,785 --> 00:06:58,408
Adding the stronger link instead of the weaker one, which makes every pair look

108
00:06:58,408 --> 00:06:58,836
close.

109
00:06:58,836 --> 00:07:04,531
Forgetting the tie-break, so A, B and D, E swap places from one run to the next.

110
00:07:04,531 --> 00:07:10,368
And counting big shared nets at full weight, which glues unrelated cells together.

111
00:07:10,368 --> 00:07:14,303
Recipe one, edge weight: look only at the direct wire.

112
00:07:14,303 --> 00:07:18,747
Recipe two, shared neighbors: add the help of common friends.

113
00:07:18,747 --> 00:07:23,847
Recipe three, pin count: big shared nets count less than private ones.

114
00:07:23,847 --> 00:07:28,875
Ties are broken alphabetically, so results never change between runs.

115
00:07:28,875 --> 00:07:32,737
Different recipes can choose a different first merge.

116
00:07:32,737 --> 00:07:36,744
Choosing the recipe is a design decision, not a detail.

117
00:07:36,744 --> 00:07:42,521
Try the browser lab challenges, or write the three recipes yourself and pass the

118
00:07:42,521 --> 00:07:43,099
checker.

119
00:07:43,099 --> 00:07:46,132
Then explain two things in your own words.

120
00:07:46,132 --> 00:07:49,237
First, why does A, C climb with recipe two?

121
00:07:49,237 --> 00:07:53,498
Second, why does the priority graph change its first merge?

122
00:07:53,498 --> 00:07:57,470
Take the short quiz, then move on to greedy pair merge.

123
00:07:57,470 --> 00:08:00,936
There, these scores become the rule for merging.
